//Object Slicing

Object slicing happens when a child-class object is copied into a parent-class object by value.

Example:

class Animal {
public:
    int x = 10;
};

class Dog : public Animal {
public:
    int y = 20;
};

Now:

Dog d;
Animal a = d;

d contains:

Dog
├── x
└── y

But a is an Animal object, so only the Animal part is copied:

Animal
└── x

The Dog-specific part y is sliced off.

That's why it's called object slicing.

Important

Object slicing generally occurs when assigning/copying by value:

Animal a = d;  // slicing

It does not happen when using a pointer or reference:

Animal* a = &d;   // no slicing
Animal& b = d;    // no slicing

Simple definition:

Object slicing = when a derived-class object is copied into a base-class object by value, causing the derived-specific part to be lost.